          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Patterns in Life: Diversity and Classification"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "N 229"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.088,
            0.776,
            0.14
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "characteristics, such as drought tolerance, pest resistance and their ability to grow in nutrient poor soils. They realised that diversity reduces the risk of crop failure and strengthens food security."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.107,
            0.142,
            0.776,
            0.262
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In such a vast diversity, organisms share certain similarities while also showing many differences. To study diversity systematically, scientists group and classify organisms based on their shared characteristics and evolutionary relationships. Classification helps us understand how organisms are related, how they function and how we can use this knowledge in activities, such as ecosystem management, biodiversity conservation, sustainable farming, and so on."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.274,
            0.567,
            0.295
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "12.1 India as a Biodiversity Hotspot"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.298,
            0.545,
            0.417
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The natural landscape of our country is diverse — with mountains in the north, desert in the west, rainforests in the North East India, plateaus in the south, and long coastlines along the Arabian Sea and the Bay of Bengal. Each of these regions has distinct soil types and different climatic conditions like temperature and rainfall."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.42,
            0.545,
            0.573
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "These diverse habitats together support a wide variety of species. Some of these species are restricted to particular regions of the world and are not found naturally anywhere else. Such species are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "endemic species",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". For example, Nilgiri tahr (Fig. 12.1a), Lion-tailed macaque (Fig. 12.1b), Indian variety of the pitcher plant — Nepenthes khasiana (Fig. 12.1c) and Neelakurinji (Fig. 12.1d) are found only in India."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.575,
            0.545,
            0.694
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Regions that support a large number of endemic species and have undergone significant habitat loss are known as biodiversity hotspots. These areas are particularly important for biodiversity conservation. Regions, such as the Western Ghats, Indo-Burma (including North East India), the Himalayas, Sundaland (including"
            }
          ]
        },
